    ('9765', 4, $$Football goal post.$$),
    ('9765', 5, $$See-Saw.$$),
    ('9765', 6, $$Swing.$$),
    ('9766', 0, $$Once there was a young man called Kumbana.$$),
    ('9766', 1, $$When he was 25, he fell in love with a girl called Zangane.$$),
    ('9766', 2, $$They were together for 3 years, but he didn't have enough money to marry her.$$),
    ('9766', 3, $$Then one day she found she was pregnant, because they didn't use protection.$$),
    ('9766', 4, $$A little girl was born, and they called her Kheri.$$),
    ('9766', 5, $$But Kumbana was still studying, and Zangane had a job in a town far away, so they had to live apart.$$),
    ('9766', 6, $$After a while, Zangane found a new boyfriend. He came to live with her and the little girl.$$),
    ('9766', 7, $$Kumbana was heart-broken. He cried and cried because he was separated from his daughter.$$),
    ('9766', 8, $$Time went on, and Kumbana found a new girlfriend too. They lived together, but they did not have any children.$$),
    ('9766', 9, $$Now Kheri did not like her new father at all. She was very unhappy, and he used to beat her.$$),
    ('9766', 10, $$So when she was 12, her mother decided to send her back to live with her real father.$$),
    ('9766', 11, $$Then Kumbana and Kheri were both happy again.$$),
    ('9787', 0, $$Long time ago, there were two young men who lost their cows. They looked for the cows until it was late.$$),
    ('9787', 1, $$As it got dark, they saw a light in a house. They agreed to go and ask for a place to sleep.$$),
    ('9787', 2, $$They went to the house and knocked. The door opened and they went in. The young men didn't see anyone. But they heard a voice greeting them.$$),
    ('9787', 3, $$The voice said, "I am a louse. Open the metal pot and have food. Open the earthenware pot and drink amahewu. "$$),
    ('9787', 4, $$Then the louse put on its skin and went out.$$),
    ('9787', 5, $$The young men ate, drank, and gave thanks. Then they left the house.$$),
    ('9787', 6, $$As they left, they had a bad thought. They said, "No, it couldn't have happened that we had food from a louse. " Eventually they agreed to go back and crush the louse.$$),
    ('9787', 7, $$They went back to the house and crushed the louse. Suddenly the louse was gone, and the house disappeared! They found themselves outside in the open.$$),
    ('9792', 0, $$Keke sat on her swing. She loved her swing. It was the best swing at her school.$$),
    ('9792', 1, $$Keke moved slowly on her swing. Backwards and forwards, backwards and forwards. When Keke moved, her swing went faster and faster. Soon she was right at the top.$$),
    ('9792', 2, $$Keke looked down at the ground. She was very high up. "Aeei," she called, "Look how high I am! "$$),
    ('9792', 3, $$She was happy to be so high. She could see the whole village. She was nearly as high as birds flying over the cornfields.$$),
    ('9792', 4, $$After a while Keke wanted to come down. The swing would not stop. "Oh, oh, oh," she called. "I don't know how to stop this swing! "$$),
    ('9792', 5, $$The swing went on and on. Up and down, up and down. Keke was frightened. "Where will this swing take me? I might go up to the moon. I must get off! " she cried.$$),
    ('9792', 6, $$So, Keke let go of the swing. She fell down, down, down.$$),
    ('9792', 7, $$"Oh, oh, oh! " cried the other children. Keke fell onto the ground. She hurt her head. "Eeeeh, eeeeh, eeeh," she cried.$$),
    ('9792', 8, $$The other children were very sad. Keke did not know what to do. "Eeeeh, eeeeh, eeeh," she cried.$$),
    ('9792', 9, $$The children called their teacher. "M'e Mpho. Please come! " they called. "Keke fell off the swing. She has hurt her head. "$$),
    ('9792', 10, $$M'e Mpho ran to the swing. She picked Keke up off the ground. M'e Mpho kissed Keke's head. Keke smiled, "That's better now. " "I will not go on that swing by myself again," she said. "I never want to go to the moon. "$$),
    ('9792', 11, $$"Next time I will be with you on your swing, Keke," said M'e Mpho. "We can go to the moon together! "$$),
    ('9797', 0, $$This is a girl called Norah. Her doll's name is Selah.$$),
    ('9797', 1, $$Norah loves her doll very much. Norah plays with Selah every day. She sleeps with her doll every night.$$),
    ('9797', 2, $$One day, Norah could not find her doll. She looked everywhere, but could not find Selah. Norah got very worried.$$),
    ('9797', 3, $$Norah did not know that her mother took Selah for washing. She hung the doll outside to dry.$$),
    ('9797', 4, $$"I can't find my doll," cried Norah. "Don't worry," said her mother.$$),
    ('9797', 5, $$That evening, Norah's mother gave the doll to Norah. The doll was very clean. Selah looked like a new doll!$$),
    ('9797', 6, $$Norah was very happy. She jumped up and down. She danced. She laughed loudly.$$),
    ('9797', 7, $$And she thanked her mother.$$),
    ('9809', 0, $$In a village on the slopes of Mount Kenya in East Africa, a little girl worked in the fields with her mother. Her name was Wangari.$$),
    ('9809', 1, $$Wangari loved being outside. In her family's food garden she broke up the soil with her machete. She pressed tiny seeds into the warm earth.$$),
    ('9809', 2, $$Her favourite time of day was just after sunset. When it got too dark to see the plants, Wangari knew it was time to go home. She would follow the narrow paths through the fields, crossing rivers as she went.$$),
    ('9809', 3, $$Wangari was a clever child and couldn't wait to go to school. But her mother and father wanted her to stay and help them at home. When she was seven years old, her big brother persuaded her parents to let her go to school.$$),
    ('9809', 4, $$She liked to learn! Wangari learnt more and more with every book she read. She did so well at school that she was invited to study in the United States of America. Wangari was excited! She wanted to know more about the world.$$),
    ('9809', 5, $$At the American university Wangari learnt many new things. She studied plants and how they grow. And she remembered how she grew: playing games with her brothers in the shade of the trees in the beautiful Kenyan forests.$$),
    ('9809', 6, $$The more she learnt, the more she realised that she loved the people of Kenya. She wanted them to be happy and free. The more she learnt, the more she remembered her African home.$$),
    ('9809', 7, $$When she had finished her studies, she returned to Kenya. But her country had changed. Huge farms stretched across the land. Women had no wood to make cooking fires. The people were poor and the children were hungry.$$),
    ('9809', 8, $$Wangari knew what to do. She taught the women how to plant trees from seeds. The women sold the trees and used the money to look after their families. The women were very happy. Wangari had helped them to feel powerful and strong.$$),
    ('9809', 9, $$As time passed, the new trees grew into forests, and the rivers started flowing again. Wangari's message spread across Africa. Today, millions of trees have grown from Wangari's seeds.$$),
    ('9809', 10, $$Wangari had worked hard. People all over the world took notice, and gave her a famous prize. It is called the Nobel Peace Prize, and she was the first African woman ever to receive it.$$),
    ('9809', 11, $$Wangari died in 2011, but we can think of her every time we see a beautiful tree.$$),
    ('9825', 0, $$We love soccer.$$),
    ('9825', 1, $$Moloko is teaching us soccer.$$),
    ('9825', 2, $$We are playing soccer.$$),
    ('9825', 3, $$Moloko is the best teacher!$$),
    ('9825', 4, $$After the game, Moloko told us that she has a surprise for us.$$),
    ('9825', 5, $$But she will only let us know tomorrow.$$),
    ('9825', 6, $$The next day we all came early to the field. We all love surprises.$$),
    ('9825', 7, $$Moloko has new soccer kits and balls for all of us! What a great surprise!$$),
    ('9830', 0, $$"No Nomvula, don't play with water. It is so scarce! " "But it's hot, Mama! I feel like getting into this water. I want to be cool. "$$),
    ('9830', 1, $$"No, Nomvula. Don't play with water. Water is scarce. " "But it's hot, Mama, I'm thirsty. I'd like to drink cold water. "$$),
    ('9830', 2, $$It is raining. There is going to be a lot of water. Now I can't play – I wish it could be hot.$$),
    ('9830', 3, $$Look at the vegetables. They are growing and becoming green. Look! The bowls and buckets are overflowing with water.$$),
    ('9830', 4, $$It has stopped raining. Now we can play! Look how beautiful everything is after the rain. The sky is clear and blue. Trees and grass are green, and the plants are flowering.$$),
    ('9830', 5, $$Oh no! There the rain is again! When are we going to play?$$),
    ('9830', 6, $$Oh no, me! I'm going to play, even if it's raining! Oh it's so lovely to play in the rain. Chapha chapha chapha! Imanz'ilokhwe yam! Imanz'ilokhwe yam! Chapha chapha chapha! Zimanz'iinwele zam! Zimanz'iinwele zam!$$),
    ('9830', 7, $$Nomvula, you don't want to listen, my child. Come closer, let me dry and comb your hair. Chapha chapha chapha! Zimanz'iinwele zakho! Zimanz'iinwele zakho!$$),
    ('988', 0, $$It was a very hot Saturday afternoon in December.$$),
    ('988', 1, $$"Bontle and Mpho and Lerato, go out and play! " Mme said to us. "I don't want you under my feet. " We ran out of the house.$$),
    ('988', 2, $$"Let's go to the river," Lerato said. "It's cooler there. " "But Mme told us not to swim in the river," said Bontle. "We won't swim," answered Lerato. "We'll just play in the shade next to the water. "$$),
    ('988', 3, $$But playing libeke is hot work, even when you're under the trees next to the river. First we took off our shoes. But we were still hot. Then we took off our shirts and skirts. But we were still hot.$$),
    ('988', 4, $$We put our feet in the river to cool off. Then we splashed each other. Soon we were soaked with water.$$),
    ('988', 5, $$"Oh come on! Let's swim," said Mpho. "Mme will never know. " We swam and swam and forgot about the time.$$),
    ('988', 6, $$The sun started to go down, and the day began to cool. Where were our clothes?$$),
    ('988', 7, $$We looked under the trees. We looked on the bushes. We looked everywhere.$$),
    ('988', 8, $$There were some cows near the river, enjoying the sweet grass. Bontle looked up, "Look at that cow! What's in her mouth? " "She's eating a red flower," said Lerato. "It's not a red flower," shouted Mpho. "It's your shirt! "$$),
    ('988', 9, $$We looked at another cow who was chewing something blue. "That's my skirt! " shouted Bontle.$$),
    ('988', 10, $$We went home in our panties, shivering. But not only because it was cool. "It was the cows," we cried. "The cows ate our clothes. "$$),
    ('988', 11, $$But did Mme believe us? Soon our bottoms were very warm. And it wasn't from the sun.$$),
    ('9881', 0, $$Long time ago, there were four little friends namely: Joel, Jonah, Job and Jacob.$$),
    ('9881', 1, $$They were studying at Busolwe primary school.$$),
    ('9881', 2, $$The boys used to go to school with out taking tea.$$),
    ('9881', 3, $$They used to feel sleepy at school all the because of hunger.$$),
    ('9881', 4, $$One day, they decided to go to to a nearby trading center called gampe and look for something to eat.$$),
    ('9881', 5, $$After crossing to a nearby trading center, they saw a bigger banana bunch in a box at the veranda.$$),
    ('9881', 6, $$The boys whispered at each other hahahaaaaa. We have been very hungry but, said Jonah.$$),
    ('9881', 7, $$All ran to the veranda. Picked up some bananas from the box, smiled and started eating. Do you now what they forgot to do?$$),
    ('9881', 8, $$While eating, the owner of the bananas came walking in bigger strides. ''Who has given you my bananas? '' asked the man.$$),
    ('9881', 9, $$As soon as they heard that voice, the boys ran as fast as they could and hid somewhere. They said to each other that no laughing, no coughing and no talking.$$),
    ('9881', 10, $$The owner of the bananas made an alarm, ''wulululululua, here are the thieves.$$),
    ('9881', 11, $$The boys were arrested, taken to school, called their parents, the teachers, and the school children to gather.$$),
    ('9881', 12, $$The boys were given a punishment. Each got some ten strokes and repented not to steal anything again.$$),
    ('9902', 0, $$Imagine a day. When we could have a picnic on the shores of Lake Victoria.$$),
    ('9902', 1, $$Imagine a day. When everyone can read and there are books in every language.$$),
    ('9902', 2, $$Imagine a day. When I shall give birth to my first baby.$$),
    ('9902', 3, $$Imagine a day. When all people in the world live peacefully as brothers and sisters.$$),
    ('9902', 4, $$Imagine a day. When I can walk on water.$$),
    ('9902', 5, $$Imagine a day. When your daughter becomes a president.$$),
    ('9902', 6, $$Imagine a day. When you are about to be put in a grave and then you come back to life again!$$),
    ('9902', 7, $$Imagine a day. When people develop wings to fly, and reach where their creator is.$$),
    ('9902', 8, $$Imagine a day. When all the ground is covered with a carpet across the whole world.$$),
    ('9966', 0, $$Chairs and tables.$$),
    ('9966', 1, $$A lot of story books for children and adults.$$),
    ('9966', 2, $$Painted pictures on the wall.$$),
    ('9966', 3, $$White and blue clouds on the ceiling board.$$),
    ('9966', 4, $$Children and adults go to the library.$$),
    ('9966', 5, $$My friends and I go to the library in holidays.$$),
    ('9966', 6, $$We read this book on dear day.$$),
    ('9966', 7, $$My mother borrowed a picture book from the library.$$),
    ('9966', 8, $$A place for me.$$),
    ('9966', 9, $$a place for you.$$),
    ('9966', 10, $$a place for all.$$),
    ('9966', 11, $$i enjoy the library so muuuuuuuuch!$$),
    ('9968', 0, $$Today I went to the zoo.$$),
    ('9968', 1, $$I saw a lot. I saw a lion.$$),
    ('9968', 2, $$I saw an elephant.$$),
    ('9968', 3, $$I saw a crocodile.$$),
    ('9968', 4, $$I saw giraffes.$$),
    ('9968', 5, $$I saw a lot of zebras.$$),
    ('9968', 6, $$I even saw a cow.$$),
    ('9968', 7, $$It was a lot of fun. But then Daddy drove us home.$$),
    ('9984', 0, $$My little brother sleeps late. I wake early, because I am great!$$),
    ('9984', 1, $$I am the one who lets in the sun.$$),
    ('9984', 2, $$"You're my morning star," says Ma.$$),
    ('9984', 3, $$I wash myself every day, I don't need any help.$$),
    ('9984', 4, $$I don't mind cold water, or the blue soap that washes clothes.$$),
    ('9984', 5, $$Ma reminds, "Don't forget your teeth. " I reply, "Never, not me! "$$),
    ('9984', 6, $$After washing, I greet Grandpa and Auntie. I wish them a good day.$$),
    ('9984', 7, $$Then I dress myself. "I'm big now, Ma," I say.$$),
    ('9984', 8, $$I can close my buttons and buckle my shoes.$$),
    ('9984', 9, $$I make sure my brother knows all the school news.$$),
    ('9984', 10, $$In class I do my best in every way.$$),
    ('9984', 11, $$I do all these good things every day. But the thing I like most, is to play and play!$$),
    ('9985', 0, $$There was a great builder in Kabubbu village.$$),
    ('9985', 1, $$He built many houses in the village.$$),
    ('9985', 2, $$But he was sleeping in a bad hut.$$),
    ('9985', 3, $$People asked about this hut every day. "Don't worry, I have a plan," the builder always replied.$$),
    ('9985', 4, $$He saved his money and bought bricks.$$),
    ('9985', 5, $$Then he bought sand, cement, iron sheets, and nails.$$),
    ('9985', 6, $$Gradually, he built himself a large, beautiful house.$$),
    ('9985', 7, $$His friends rejoiced to see this new house.$$),
    ('9985', 8, $$He prepared for a party and invited everyone. They ate and drank.$$),
    ('9985', 9, $$Then they danced.$$),
    ('9985', 10, $$The builder received many gifts for his new home.$$),
    ('9985', 11, $$Now the builder in Kabubbu village sleeps in a good house.$$)
  ) AS v(source_id, page_index, text)
  JOIN books b
    ON b.source_platform = 'african_storybook' AND b.source_id = v.source_id
ON CONFLICT (book_id, page_index) DO NOTHING;

-- ───────── [적재검증] 트랜잭션 안 ─────────
-- (c) 적재 후 행 수 — 기대 464
SELECT count(*) AS rows_after FROM book_text bt
  JOIN books b ON b.id = bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('40344', '40360', '40777', '5266', '5377', '5394', '5397', '5413', '5566', '6165', '6369', '6895', '7387', '7555', '7590', '7652', '7656', '7769', '8220', '8487', '8554', '8815', '8950', '920', '9208', '9266', '9299', '9545', '9663', '9668', '9702', '9733', '9765', '9766', '9787', '9792', '9797', '9809', '9825', '9830', '988', '9881', '9902', '9966', '9968', '9984', '9985');
-- (d) 조인 실패로 누락된 source_id — 기대 0행
SELECT v.source_id FROM (VALUES ('40344'), ('40360'), ('40777'), ('5266'), ('5377'), ('5394'), ('5397'), ('5413'), ('5566'), ('6165'), ('6369'), ('6895'), ('7387'), ('7555'), ('7590'), ('7652'), ('7656'), ('7769'), ('8220'), ('8487'), ('8554'), ('8815'), ('8950'), ('920'), ('9208'), ('9266'), ('9299'), ('9545'), ('9663'), ('9668'), ('9702'), ('9733'), ('9765'), ('9766'), ('9787'), ('9792'), ('9797'), ('9809'), ('9825'), ('9830'), ('988'), ('9881'), ('9902'), ('9966'), ('9968'), ('9984'), ('9985')) AS v(source_id)
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM books b
     WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id=v.source_id);
-- (e) source 라벨 분포 — 기대 manifest_txt_v1 1종 / 464행
SELECT bt.source, count(*) FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('40344', '40360', '40777', '5266', '5377', '5394', '5397', '5413', '5566', '6165', '6369', '6895', '7387', '7555', '7590', '7652', '7656', '7769', '8220', '8487', '8554', '8815', '8950', '920', '9208', '9266', '9299', '9545', '9663', '9668', '9702', '9733', '9765', '9766', '9787', '9792', '9797', '9809', '9825', '9830', '988', '9881', '9902', '9966', '9968', '9984', '9985')
  GROUP BY bt.source;
-- (f) page_index 축 검증 — 권마다 0부터 연속이어야 한다. 기대 0행(위반 없음)
SELECT b.source_id, min(bt.page_index) AS mn, max(bt.page_index) AS mx, count(*) AS n
  FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('40344', '40360', '40777', '5266', '5377', '5394', '5397', '5413', '5566', '6165', '6369', '6895', '7387', '7555', '7590', '7652', '7656', '7769', '8220', '8487', '8554', '8815', '8950', '920', '9208', '9266', '9299', '9545', '9663', '9668', '9702', '9733', '9765', '9766', '9787', '9792', '9797', '9809', '9825', '9830', '988', '9881', '9902', '9966', '9968', '9984', '9985')
  GROUP BY b.source_id HAVING min(bt.page_index) <> 0 OR max(bt.page_index) <> count(*)-1;
-- (g) 빈 면 행 수 — 기대 0 (D7: 빈 면도 text='' 로 적재)
SELECT count(*) AS empty_rows FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('40344', '40360', '40777', '5266', '5377', '5394', '5397', '5413', '5566', '6165', '6369', '6895', '7387', '7555', '7590', '7652', '7656', '7769', '8220', '8487', '8554', '8815', '8950', '920', '9208', '9266', '9299', '9545', '9663', '9668', '9702', '9733', '9765', '9766', '9787', '9792', '9797', '9809', '9825', '9830', '988', '9881', '9902', '9966', '9968', '9984', '9985') AND bt.text = '';

-- ───────── [종료] ─────────
-- (c)~(g)가 전부 기대값과 일치하면 아래 ROLLBACK; 을 COMMIT; 으로 바꿔 타이핑한다.
ROLLBACK;
